# Hygiene allowlist & rationale (companion to test/hygiene.sh)
#
# This file documents the exemption rules so a flagged contributor (or agent)
# knows what passed and why. It is NOT a general-purpose ignore list: add an
# entry only for a false positive you can explain, and record the reason here.

## Exempt in the script (by rule, not by content)
#
# 1. docs/adr/0014-public-local-boundary.md — the policy ADR itself; it quotes
#    the forbidden patterns in prose.
# 2. test/hygiene.sh and this file — the enforcement mechanism and its
#    documentation.
# 3. Any line containing the word "hygiene" — i.e. discussion about the check.
#    This keeps changelog/PR text like "hygiene job added" exempt even if it
#    mentions an IP convention.
#
# Rejected exemptions (on purpose):
# - RFC5737/RFC2606 example ranges (192.0.2.x, 198.51.100.x, 203.0.113.x,
#   example.com) need no exemption — they are outside the flagged RFC1918/
#   CGNAT ranges, so fixtures using them simply pass.
# - "prose explanations inside code" — no; if a tracked file needs a real
#   private IP it must be generalized, per ADR-0014 §3.

## Known benign patterns
#
# - "`top-homelab`-style" — docs (service-spec.md, m4 milestone) use this as an
#   explicitly fictional label describing the kind of repo users have; no
#   hosting data accompanies it. The hygiene script exempts this hyphenated
#   form only; a bare "top-homelab" is still flagged.
# - Version triples like "0.10.2" or "go1.24" never match because the IP regex
#   requires three dotted numeric octets bounded by non-digit characters.

## How to add a temporary exemption (and how to retire it)
#
# Prefer narrowing the matched *line* (e.g. a prose sentence in a policy doc)
# over widening the script. If you must exempt content, put it behind a
# clearly-named variable in the doc and list it here with a retirement date.
